"A network segmentation map groups workloads into security zones and states which directional connections policy allows.\n\nUse when: Review east-west access and network isolation. Explain intended allow paths and default-deny boundaries.\n\nAvoid when: Physical cabling or precise routing tables are the main concern.\n\nVisual grammar: Padded regions identify security zones or selector-defined workload groups. Directed links name allowed protocol and destination-port combinations. Policy annotations distinguish ingress, egress, default rules, and enforcement points.\n\nBuild: Choose an environment and identify policy enforcement technology. Group workloads by actual selectors or policy boundaries. Draw explicit allowed flows and documented default behavior. Reserve corridors between zones so rule labels never sit on border crossings.\n\nCheck: Ingress and egress rules are evaluated under the actual platform semantics. Selectors match the intended workloads. A line represents policy allowance rather than guaranteed application success. Denied and unknown paths are distinguishable without cluttering labels.\n\nPitfalls: Treating a subnet boundary as an enforced security policy by itself. Assuming a network policy controls traffic the selected implementation does not enforce.\n\nExample: Frontend may call API on one port; API may reach Database, while other cross-zone flows remain denied by the stated policy.\n\nAlternatives: Logical network diagram, Threat-model data-flow diagram."
